ach index, giving about N\u00b2/2 operations \u2014 roughly 200ms at N=2000, which interestingly lines up with the baseline p99 too.\n\nNow I need to figure out which code path actually executes when the flag is on: in render_spend_summary, typical_spend takes priority over monthly_summary if both flags are set, and monthly_summary is linear and fast, so flipping that flag alone should help \u2014 unless the actual flag controls typical_spend instead. The docstring hints are pointing at typical_spend as the newest, still-rolling-out feature whose cost \"follows the shape of the code\" \u2014 a clear nod to algorithmic complexity \u2014 while the monthly_statement docstring claims it's the one gated by \"monthly-spend-feature.\" I need to pin down which flag name actually maps to which function.\n\nThat explains it\u2014the spend figure is cache-backed for most calls, but the statement recomputes from scratch every time, walking the full history repeatedly with no caching. Turning that feature on means every long-history account pays that uncached cost, which would show up as tail latency. The fix would be collapsing those repeated per-item walks (biggest/smallest/mean each scanning independently) into a single pass, which could plausibly yield the kind of 25x speedup needed to go from over a second down to tens of milliseconds.\n\nI want to check the test conventions for the monthly statement and typical spend to see if there's an established performance-test pattern, and also look more carefully at how the statement is invoked from the account page to make sure I'm not missing another hidden cost. I should also double check what the columns in that error-rate CSV actually represent before drawing conclusions from it.\n\nLooking at the numbers again, p90 stays roughly flat across runs while p99 jumps dramatically \u2014 that pattern only shows up if the extra cost concentrates in a small tail of accounts with unusually long histories, which points toward something q